cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "OISST Fourier Neural Operator: scale-resolved evaluation of FNO forecast skill against persistence"
type: software
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-08-18
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/DiogoRibeiro7/oisst-fourier-neural-operator"
url: "https://github.com/DiogoRibeiro7/oisst-fourier-neural-operator"
authors:
  - family-names: Ribeiro
    given-names: Diogo
    affiliation: "ESMAD - Instituto Politécnico do Porto"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-2022-7072"
abstract: >-
  A notebook-first data science project asking when a Fourier Neural Operator (FNO)
  provides forecast information beyond persistence for real sea-surface temperature
  fields, and at which spatial scales that gain occurs. The study uses NOAA/NCEI
  Optimum Interpolation Sea Surface Temperature (OISST) v2.1 over the Northeast
  Atlantic and evaluates 7-day-ahead field forecasting from the previous 14 daily SST
  fields, with strictly temporal splits and preprocessing fit on training data only.
  The FNO is benchmarked against seasonal climatology, persistence (the primary null
  model), linear trend, an EOF+ridge global low-rank linear model, and a small local
  CNN, using masked RMSE, MAE, anomaly correlation, persistence skill, paired daily
  RMSE differences with block-bootstrap uncertainty, spatial error maps, and
  scale-resolved spectral error energy. The repository claims no novel architecture and
  no first application of FNOs to ocean or Atlantic SST forecasting; its contribution is
  the diagnostic design that attributes skill by spatial-frequency band, season, and
  forecast-difficulty regime, and it treats a negative result as a valid outcome.
keywords:
  - Fourier neural operator
  - neural operator
  - sea surface temperature
  - OISST
  - ocean forecasting
  - spectral analysis
  - scientific machine learning
  - deep learning
  - PyTorch
  - persistence baseline
  - forecast verification
  - reproducible research
